  <!-- 1080p vs 4K -->
  <h2 id="1080p-vs-4k">1080p vs 4K: What's the Difference in Meetings?</h2>
  <p>
    For most video calls, the difference is minimal. Here's why:
  </p>

  <h3>Video Call Limitations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zoom:</strong> Maximum 1080p with paid plans, 720p on free</li>
    <li><strong>Google Meet:</strong> Up to 1080p (typically lower)</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Teams:</strong> Up to 1080p</li>
    <li><strong>Bandwidth:</strong> Most calls auto-reduce quality anyway</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When 4K Matters</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Recording video for YouTube or courses</li>
    <li>Cropping and reframing in post-production</li>
    <li>Using webcam for photography/scanning</li>
    <li>Future-proofing as platforms improve</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Our advice:</strong> A quality 1080p webcam (Logitech C920s, Elgato Facecam) serves most remote workers
    perfectly. Invest in 4K only if you create video content or want maximum future-proofing.
  </p>

  <!-- Built-in vs External -->
  <h2 id="builtin-vs-external">Built-in vs External Webcam: When to Upgrade</h2>
  <p>
    Modern laptop webcams have improved, but dedicated webcams still offer advantages:
  </p>

  <h3>Upgrade If:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your laptop has 720p webcam (many still do)</li>
    <li>You're on video calls daily for work</li>
    <li>Your webcam angle looks up your nose (laptop below eye level)</li>
    <li>Colleagues complain about your video quality</li>
    <li>You work in challenging lighting conditions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Built-in May Be Fine If:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You have a MacBook with M-series chip (1080p cameras)</li>
    <li>Video calls are occasional, not daily</li>
    <li>You value portability over quality</li>
    <li>Your laptop is at eye level (external monitor setup)</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Lighting Tips -->
  <h2 id="lighting-tips">Lighting Tips for Better Video Calls</h2>
  <p>
    Lighting matters more than webcam quality. A $300 webcam with bad lighting looks worse than a $70 webcam
    with good lighting.
  </p>

  <h3>Best Practices</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Face a window:</strong> Natural light is most flattering</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid backlight:</strong> Never sit with bright light behind you</li>
    <li><strong>45° angle:</strong> Place lights at 45° to your face, slightly above eye level</li>
    <li><strong>Diffuse harsh light:</strong> Soft light is more flattering than direct</li>
    <li><strong>Check your background:</strong> Ensure lighting reveals a professional space</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Affordable Lighting Solutions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Desk lamp:</strong> Simple, effective, $20-40</li>
    <li><strong>Ring light:</strong> Even face lighting, $30-80</li>
    <li><strong>Key light:</strong> Professional option, $50-150</li>
    <li><strong>Natural light:</strong> Free, best quality</li>
  </ul>

